<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kulturkampf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kulturkampf was a political conflict in the 1870s between the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> in the newly founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. Laws restricted the Church's role in education and appointments, reflecting suspicion of Catholic loyalties. The campaign largely failed, strengthening the Catholic Centre Party, and was gradually wound down.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Kulturkampf, or cultural struggle, was fought between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> under Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a>. It was about the church's direct control of education and of ecclesiastical appointments. The years 1871 to 1878 mark only its culmination in Prussia, for the dispute over the separation of church and state was older. Predominantly Catholic Baden had been in the forefront of curbing church power, in the Baden Church Dispute of 1852 to 1854 and in its own Kulturkampf from 1864 to 1876. The name itself came into use on 17 January 1873, when the Progressive deputy Rudolf Virchow spoke in the Prussian legislature of a great cultural struggle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From 1871 to 1876 the Prussian parliament and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a>, both with liberal majorities, passed 22 laws. The Pulpit Law of 10 December 1871 punished clerics who discussed state affairs in a way that endangered public peace. The School Supervision Act of 11 March 1872, central to the campaign, ended church oversight of Prussian primary schools. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> suspended relations with the Holy See in December 1872, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> had rejected Cardinal Hohenlohe as German ambassador. Civil registration of births, marriages and deaths followed in 1874 for Prussia and in 1875 for the empire.</p><p>On 13 July 1874 Eduard Kullmann tried to shoot Bismarck at Bad Kissingen, citing the church laws, and the attempt led to harsher measures. In 1875 the encyclical Quod Nunquam declared the May Laws invalid, the Payment Law stopped state payments to clerics who would not accept all the laws, and the Congregations Law dissolved all orders except those caring for the infirm. Even many liberals saw the later laws as an encroachment on civil liberties. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-posen">Province of Posen</a> the conflict took on a more nationalistic character, with Bismarck attempting to Germanise Polish areas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The laws proved ineffective and even counterproductive against the Centre Party. The British ambassador Odo Russell reported in October 1872 that the German bishops, once politically powerless, had become powerful leaders and enthusiastic defenders of the Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Strict supervision of schools was applied only in Catholic areas, which alienated Protestant conservatives as well.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> formed the Dual Alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1879, the danger of war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> receded, and Bismarck turned to the popularity of the socialists and to import duties, on which he could not rely on the liberals. He had never been comfortable with the growing ferocity of the campaign, and he had achieved more separation of church and state than he wanted. In later usage the term describes any conflict between a secular government and religious authorities, or between factions holding opposed values.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kulturkampf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bismarck faced Catholic opposition during the <b>Kulturkampf</b>, socialist agitation from a rapidly urbanising working class, and the tension of governing through an elected parliament under monarchical authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
